review_comments.body:
**`remove_by_sandbox` has a TOCTOU gap — not atomic**

It collects session IDs via `iter()`, then removes each individually. Between the scan and removals, a concurrent handler could **insert a new session** for the same sandbox. That session would be orphaned — it remains in the tracker while its sandbox is being destroyed.

**Suggestion:** Use DashMap's `retain()` for atomic remove-by-predicate:

```rust
pub fn remove_by_sandbox(&self, sandbox_id: &str) -> Vec<TerminalSession> {
    let mut removed = Vec::new();
    self.sessions.retain(|_, session| {
        if session.sandbox_id == sandbox_id {
            removed.push(session.clone());
            false
        } else {
            true
        }
    });
    removed
}
```

This also makes session enumeration O(1) as a side benefit.

